# Paper Folding and Cutting

> A square sheet is folded twice, a corner is punched through all
> layers, and the paper is unfolded. How many holes, and where?
> Paper-folding questions are pure symmetry bookkeeping: every fold
> creates a mirror, every cut pierces every layer, and unfolding
> reflects the cut once per fold.

## 1. The Core Principle

[definition]
When a sheet is folded, the fold line becomes an **axis of
symmetry**: whatever is cut through the folded stack appears on
**every layer**. Unfolding the sheet therefore **reflects the cut
across every fold line**, once for each fold made, producing the
final pattern of holes.
[/definition]

Two consequences drive every question:

1. **Holes multiply by layers.** A punch through 4 layers = 4 holes
   when opened.
2. **Position comes from reflection.** Each hole's partner lies
   mirrored across the fold line, at the same perpendicular distance
   on the other side.

## 2. Tracking Folds: The Half-Turn Table

Folds double the layers (for folds that stack the paper over
itself):

| Folds | Layers | Holes per punch |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 | 1 |
| 1 | 2 | 2 |
| 2 | 4 | 4 |
| 3 | 8 | 8 |

But position matters more than count: a punch **on** the fold line
punches only the doubled edge and produces a hole *bisected* by the
fold line (a half-hole becomes a full hole straddling the axis on
opening). A punch at a **corner made by two folds** multiplies
across both axes.

```text
Fold once vertically, punch top-right corner:

  +------+--/|--+         +------+------+
  |      |  X   |   ==>   |   X  |  X   |
  |      |      |  open   |      |      |
  +------+------+         +------+------+
        (fold)          two holes, mirrored
```

## 3. Standard Question Types

**(a) Find the punched paper** — you are shown the folded paper with
punched holes, and must pick the unfolded sheet. Method: mentally
unfold **one fold at a time, in reverse order of folding** (last
fold first), reflecting all holes across that fold line.

**(b) Find the punched position** — the final unfolded pattern is
given; find which folded-punch would produce it. Method: fold the
pattern back down, mirroring hole-groups across the fold lines
until the paper is the given folded size; the surviving hole is the
punch position.

**(c) Fold-direction questions** — the fold sequence is described in
words ("folded twice, first vertically, then horizontally, then
punched at the top-left of the remaining stack"). Draw a small
diagram with the stack shrinking each fold; the punch position in
the question always refers to the FINAL stacked shape.

[important]
Unfold in **reverse order**: if the paper was folded right-over-left
and then bottom-over-top, you open the horizontal fold first (holes
reflect top-bottom), then the vertical fold (holes reflect
left-right). Reversing this order is the number-one error in the
chapter.
[/important]

## 4. Worked Examples

**Example 1 — two folds, one punch.** A square is folded
vertically (right half onto left), then horizontally (bottom half
onto top). The top-left corner of the resulting quarter is punched.
Unfold the horizontal fold first: the corner hole gains a mirror
below the horizontal axis — two holes at top-left and bottom-left
of the half-sheet. Now unfold the vertical axis: each hole gains a
mirror to the right — four holes, one in each corner. Answer:
**4 corner holes** (diagonally symmetric pattern: all four corners).

**Example 2 — punch on the fold.** One vertical fold; the punch
lands **on** the folded edge. Opening gives a single hole centred
ON the crease line — the punch went through both layers at the same
spot, and the two half-holes merge. Count: **1 hole**, straddling
the fold line.

**Example 3 — cut shapes, not holes.** A folded paper has a
triangular notch cut from the folded edge. Unfolding reveals the
full symmetric shape the notch traces: a **rhombus/diamond** (two
mirrored triangles meeting at the crease), or if cut from a corner,
an arrowhead. Cutting questions obey the same reflection law as
punch questions; only the shapes are richer.

## 5. Multiple Cuts and Mixed Patterns

With two or more punches at different positions, track each punch
independently — patterns don't interact unless their mirror images
overlap (two holes may merge into one larger/odd hole; exams rarely
do this, but symmetry-aware merging explains such options).

Dotted-line conventions in exam figures: solid lines = visible
edges, dashed = fold creases, arrows = fold direction. Read the
creases first, decide the stacking order, then apply the reverse
unfold.

## 6. Tactics That Score

1. **Draw the stack.** Three quick rectangles, each half the
   previous size, labelled with fold order — 15 seconds that
   prevent most errors.
2. **Unfold in reverse.** Last fold made is first fold opened.
3. **Count first, position second.** The hole count (layers)
   eliminates half the options instantly.
4. **Punch on a crease = merged hole on the line**; punch on a
   corner made by creases = symmetric cluster around that corner.
5. **For notch/slit cuts**, think "what symmetric shape do the
   mirrored cut-outs combine into?"
6. **Verify with one mirror:** the final pattern must be symmetric
   across EVERY fold line — if an option breaks the crease
   symmetry, it is wrong regardless of hole count.

## 7. Summary

Paper folding is reflection made physical: each fold is a mirror,
each punch prints on every layer, and unfolding runs the mirror
back. Count layers for the number of holes; unfold in reverse fold
order for their positions; respect crease symmetry in the final
pattern. With the stack-diagram habit, the whole chapter reduces to
ten seconds of drawing and one careful reflection per fold.
